```cpp
Point global;
Point foo_bar(Point arg)
{
	Point local = arg, *heap = new Point(global);
	*heap = local;
	Point pa[ 4 ] = { local, *heap };
	return *heap;
}
```

The copy constructor is used in the following statements:

```text
Point foo_bar(Point arg):
```

arg is passed by value, which means it is copy initialized.

```text
Point local = arg,
```

local is copy initialized from arg.

```cpp
*heap = new Point(global);
```

heap is a pointer to Point that is copy initialized from the new expression. Essentially, the pointer returned from new is used to copy initialized local (copied to local).

```cpp
Point pa[ 4 ] = { local, *heap };
```

pa is an array of 4 elements of type Point that is brace initialized. Copy constructor is used to copy initialize pa from the braced list.

```cpp
return *heap;
```

foo_bar returns a non-reference value, the copy constructor is used to initialize a copy of the returned value *heap.

